For this Improvement Action Agent how do the MCP tools call will be made? Is it through http?
Good question. Let me trace the exact call path through the code so the answer is precise.

Read c:\Thomas\IK-CapStone-Project\ats_multi_agent_hiring\mcp_platform\mcp\mcp_client.py
Read c:\Thomas\IK-CapStone-Project\ats_multi_agent_hiring\mcp_platform\mcp\tools\search_similar_interventions.py
Read c:\Thomas\IK-CapStone-Project\ats_multi_agent_hiring\vector_store\chroma\client.py
Read c:\Thomas\IK-CapStone-Project\ats_multi_agent_hiring\agents\insight\improvement_action\improvement_action_agent.py
Now I have the full picture. Here's the precise answer:

How the MCP Tool Calls Work — No HTTP Between Agent and Tools
The MCP tools are direct in-process Python function calls, not HTTP. Here is the exact call chain:


ImprovementActionAgent.analyse()
  └── asyncio.gather(
        mcp.fetch_evidence(issue_1),
        mcp.fetch_evidence(issue_2),
        mcp.fetch_evidence(issue_3)
      )
        └── MCPClient.fetch_evidence()
              └── asyncio.gather(
                    search_interventions(issue),   ← Python function call
                    get_best_practices(issue)       ← Python function call
                  )
Each tool is an async def in a .py file inside mcp_platform/mcp/tools/. MCPClient calls them with await — same process, same memory, no network socket.

Where HTTP Does Appear — ChromaDB Only
The one place HTTP is used is inside the tool itself, when it queries ChromaDB:


search_similar_interventions()
  ├── _get_embedder().embed(issue)
  │     └── sentence-transformers (local CPU — no HTTP)
  └── _get_collection().search_by_issue(embedding)
        └── InterventionsCollection._col.query(...)
              └── chromadb.HttpClient  ← HTTP to localhost:8001
chromadb.HttpClient in vector_store/chroma/client.py:14 opens an HTTP connection to localhost:8001 (your Docker-hosted ChromaDB). That is the only network call in the entire MCP tool path.

Comparison: This Implementation vs Real MCP Protocol
Aspect	This project	Real MCP (Model Context Protocol)
Tool invocation	Direct Python await fn()	HTTP or stdio between agent process and tool server
Tool server	No separate server — tools are library code	Separate MCP server process (e.g. mcp-server-filesystem)
Transport	In-process function call	JSON-RPC over HTTP or stdio
Discovery	Hardcoded in MCPClient	Agent asks server to list available tools at runtime
Timeout	asyncio.wait_for inside MCPClient	HTTP request timeout
The architecture is deliberately simplified for this 2-week project. The plan notes called out the same thing:

"search_similar_interventions — This is a pure ChromaDB semantic search — no external HTTP call needed."

What Would Change for True MCP
If you wanted to wire this up to a real MCP server (e.g. to share tools across multiple agent processes or call external third-party tools), you would:

Run a separate MCP tool server (e.g. using the mcp Python SDK)
Replace MCPClient.search_interventions() with an HTTP POST to http://mcp-server/tools/search_similar_interventions
The tool server receives JSON, calls the same ChromaDB logic, returns JSON
MCPClient parses the response back into CaseStudy objects
The MCPClient facade is specifically designed so that switch would only touch mcp_client.py — the agent and tools themselves would not change.

